= ACM code of ethics

=== Contribute to Society and Human Well-Being
Use computing skills to benefit society, protect human rights, minimize harm, respect diversity, and promote environmental sustainability.

=== Avoid Harm
Prevent negative consequences through careful consideration of impacts. Mitigate unintended harm and ensure intentional harm is ethically justified and minimized.

=== Be Honest and Trustworthy
Maintain transparency about system capabilities and limitations. Avoid false claims, data fabrication, and conflicts of interest.

=== Be Fair and Non-Discriminatory
Prohibit discrimination based on protected characteristics. Foster inclusive participation and design accessible technologies that don't disenfranchise people.

=== Respect Intellectual Property
Credit creators appropriately and respect copyrights, patents, and licenses. Support reasonable uses that serve the public good.

=== Respect Privacy
Collect only necessary personal information with informed consent. Implement strong data protection, enable user control, and maintain transparency about data practices.

=== Honor Confidentiality
Protect confidential information except when disclosure is necessary for law, regulations, or Code violations.

== Professional Responsibilities

=== Strive for High Quality
Deliver quality work that respects stakeholder dignity and avoids negative consequences.

=== Maintain Professional Competence
Continuously upgrade technical knowledge, social awareness, communication skills, and ethical understanding.

=== Respect Rules and Laws
Follow applicable laws, regulations, and organizational policies. Challenge unethical rules through proper channels.

=== Engage in Professional Review
Seek and provide constructive peer review at all stages of work.

=== Evaluate Systems Thoroughly
Provide objective assessments of systems, risks, and impacts. Take extraordinary care with machine learning systems.

=== Work Within Competence
Only accept assignments within areas of expertise. Disclose competency gaps to employers or clients.

=== Foster Public Understanding
Share technical knowledge appropriately and address misconceptions about computing technology.

=== Authorize Access Properly
Only access systems and data when authorized or compelled by public good.

=== Design Secure Systems
Prioritize robust, usable security. Implement monitoring, patching, and breach notification procedures.

== Professional Leadership Principles

=== Center the Public Good
Make people and public benefit the primary consideration in all computing work.

=== Promote Social Responsibility
Encourage organizational members to fulfill social responsibilities and reduce harm.

=== Enhance Working Life Quality
Support worker development, safety, well-being, dignity, and appropriate ergonomic standards.

=== Support Code-Aligned Policies
Establish and communicate organizational policies consistent with the Code. Reward compliance and address violations.

=== Create Professional Growth Opportunities
Provide educational opportunities in ethics, professionalism, and technical specialties.

=== Carefully Modify or Retire Systems
Consider user impacts when changing systems. Investigate alternatives and assist migration from legacy systems.

=== Steward Infrastructure Systems
Establish fair access policies for systems integrated into society's infrastructure. Monitor adoption levels and adapt responsibilities accordingly.

== Compliance with the Code

=== Uphold the Principles
Adhere to Code principles, improve them, and address breaches when recognized.

=== Report Violations
Consider reporting Code violations to ACM for potential remedial action.
